<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">João das Regras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1404">1404</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">João das Regras was a Portuguese jurist trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in 1385 he argued that the throne was vacant and that the rival claimants had no valid right, persuading the assembly to elect John, Master of Aviz, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> I. He then served the new king as chancellor and helped to shape royal law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joao-das-regras.jpg" alt="João das Regras" width="320" height="531" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roque Gameiro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jo%C3%A3o_das_Regras_-_Hist%C3%B3ria_de_Portugal,_popular_e_ilustrada.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
